1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,233
Before you decide what to build, find out what the problem actually is.

2
00:00:06,283 --> 00:00:08,650
That is what this does.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:13,142
You could ask a chatbot to interview your team.

4
00:00:13,192 --> 00:00:19,663
But every conversation comes out a little different, and nothing is counted across people.

5
00:00:19,713 --> 00:00:26,650
Here, everyone gets the same interview, from a template that is fixed for the campaign.

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:28,934
A colleague opens a link.

7
00:00:28,984 --> 00:00:30,522
No account, no app.

8
00:00:30,572 --> 00:00:37,666
They see who is asking, what is kept, and choose to take part with their name, or anonymously.

9
00:00:37,716 --> 00:00:39,650
Then they agree, and start.

10
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:45,909
The interviewer asks one question at a time, about how the work is really done.

11
00:00:45,959 --> 00:00:50,280
It can offer answer options, and it works on a phone.

12
00:00:50,330 --> 00:00:54,650
It is told never to ask where AI should be used.

13
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:01,650
At the end, the person sees their own summary, and can correct it.

14
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:07,067
In this demo, ten simulated colleagues from three departments took part.

15
00:01:07,117 --> 00:01:12,650
Their answers became two hundred and eighty-eight items, grouped into twenty-five themes.

16
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:18,800
Each theme is scored, and classed as AI, automation, software, or process.

17
00:01:18,850 --> 00:01:24,650
Open one, and you see the reasoning, and the quotes behind it.

18
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:28,016
Anonymity is a rule, not a promise.

19
00:01:28,066 --> 00:01:35,460
A department, a role or a quote appears only when at least three people are behind it.

20
00:01:35,510 --> 00:01:37,650
Below that, it says withheld.

21
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:42,396
The report states its method, and what it does not do.

22
00:01:42,446 --> 00:01:46,033
It can say that AI is not the answer.

23
00:01:46,083 --> 00:01:47,650
Download it as PDF.

24
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:51,913
Pick an opportunity, and start a requirements interview from it.

25
00:01:51,963 --> 00:01:58,650
What the first round learned is carried over, so the second interview asks only what is missing.

26
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:00,936
Or start from one sentence.

27
00:02:00,986 --> 00:02:03,718
A small parking tool, for clinic visitors.

28
00:02:03,768 --> 00:02:08,088
It settles the context, then decides one thing at a time.

29
00:02:08,138 --> 00:02:13,650
At the end it reads the list back, and says what is still open.

30
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:16,436
Out comes a specification.

31
00:02:16,486 --> 00:02:20,164
Numbered requirements, each with acceptance criteria.

32
00:02:20,214 --> 00:02:22,650
Marked MVP, or later.

33
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:29,519
Reopen the interview, change your mind, and you get a second version, with a diff.

34
00:02:29,569 --> 00:02:35,650
Download it as PDF, as Markdown, or as a bundle for a coding agent.

35
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:39,984
It is built to run on a server you operate.

36
00:02:40,034 --> 00:02:43,212
Every model call is recorded with its cost.

37
00:02:43,262 --> 00:02:47,650
The interface comes in English and Finnish, in light and dark.

38
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:50,976
Understand the problem, then decide what to build.

39
00:02:51,026 --> 00:02:56,650
The colleagues in this demo were simulated, and the figures come from one test run.
